  "text": "Certainly! You need a function that processes a string and returns a histogram (a dictionary) of the word frequencies. The function should:\n\n- Convert all text to **lowercase** to ensure **case-insensitive** counting.\n- Ignore **punctuation** \u2014 words should only consist of alphabetic characters.\n- Count **only the words**, ignoring any non-alphabetic characters like commas, periods, etc.\n\nHere's a well-structured and complete implementation that passes your unit tests:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Convert text to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation using string.punctuation\n    translator = str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation)\n    text = text.translate(translator)\n    \n    # Split into words and count frequency\n    words = text.split()\n    frequency = {}\n    \n    for word in words:\n        frequency[word] = frequency.get(word, 0) + 1\n    \n    return frequency\n```\n\n### \u2705 Explanation:\n\n1. **Case normalization**: `text = text.lower()` makes all letters lowercase.\n2. **Punctuation removal**: `str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation)` creates a translation table to remove all punctuation, and `text.translate(translator)` removes it.\n3. **Word splitting**: `text.split()` splits the text into words based on whitespace.\n4. **Frequency counting**: A dictionary `frequency` is used to keep track of how many times each word appears, using `.get()` to safely handle new words.\n\n### \u2705 Sample Usage:\n\n```python\nprint(word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\"))          # {\"hello\": 2, \"world\": 1}\nprint(word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\"))  # {\"python\": 2, \"is\": 2, \"great\": 1, \"fun\": 1}\nprint(word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\"))             # {\"test\": 3}\n```\n\nLet me know if you want this to handle more complex tokenization \u2014 like ignoring hyphens, apostrophes, etc.",
